Salivary Gland Stones (Sialolithiasis)
Opthalmology & ENT
Meal-induced glandular swelling, Colicky ductal pain, Palpable ductal stone

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-041
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing facial pain and severe unilateral swelling immediately following a meal, rendering the patient unable to eat or drink.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden onset of excruciating, colicky submandibular pain and rapid localized swelling immediately after eating. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute suppurative sialadenitis, impending airway compromise from deep neck space extension, and to initiate immediate mechanical and pharmacological therapy to relieve the acute ductal obstruction before routine general practice hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I took one bite of a lemon tart, and my jaw instantly swelled up with a stabbing pain so bad I couldn't chew."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced abruptly 3 hours ago during dinner. Patient reports an immediate, severe, colicky pain in the right submandibular region strongly exacerbated by the meal. Describes an episodic, rapid swelling of the affected salivary gland corresponding directly with salivary stimulation. Notes a subjective sensation of reduced salivary flow on the right side leading to localized xerostomia (dry mouth). Endorses intermittent, milder episodes of jaw ache over the past month that resolved spontaneously, but this episode is unrelenting. Denies fever, chills, or difficulty breathing.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, clutching the right jaw, refusing oral intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with spouse; high distress level due to acute pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, pacing, holding the right submandibular area.
Physical Exam: Marked, unilateral, firm swelling of the right submandibular gland. Erythema and pronounced tenderness overlie the affected gland. Intraoral bimanual palpation reveals a firm, hard, calculus mass (stone) lodged within the distal portion of Wharton's duct on the right floor of the mouth. The ductal orifice is erythematous but no frankly purulent discharge is expressible upon gentle massage. Airway is patent, tongue is midline, and there is no sublingual elevation (no Ludwig's angina). Left salivary glands are normal.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten meal on the dining table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Right Submandibular Sialolithiasis (Wharton's Duct Stone) - Status: Acute obstruction
Differentials:

Acute Suppurative Sialadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of systemic fever and the lack of expressible, frank purulent discharge from the ductal orifice, though stasis puts the patient at high risk for secondary infection.

Lymphadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strict, immediate correlation of the swelling and colicky pain with eating (salivary stimulation), and the direct palpation of a calculus in the duct.

Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal dentition on gross inspection, absence of localized gingival fluctuance, and the pain being distinctly meal-induced and colicky rather than a constant, deep ache.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic, meal-induced acute salivary colic and localized swelling. Urgent physical examination was critical to rule out a secondary deep space infection and to manually locate the obstruction. Bimanual palpation successfully identified a distal stone in Wharton's duct. By confirming a mechanical, non-infected obstruction, the urgent visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission, avoided the immediate use of broad-spectrum antibiotics, and provided targeted mechanical and analgesic strategies to flush the stone and manage the severe acute pain in the home setting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1g STAT for acute analgesia and anti-inflammatory effect. Instructed the patient on specific bimanual massage techniques (milking the duct forward).
Positive Behavioral Change: To continuously dilate the duct and flush the stone, the patient must place a sour sugar-free candy under their tongue every 2 hours while awake, immediately followed by gentle forward massage of the gland. The resulting sudden gush of salty fluid and instant relief of pressure (positive reinforcement) directly rewards the mechanical compliance needed to expel the calculus or reduce stasis.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours. If the stone does not pass spontaneously, refer to ENT or Maxillofacial surgery for sialendoscopy/excision.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops a high fever, extreme difficulty opening their mouth, swelling under the tongue that pushes it upward, or difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; ultrasound may be required if the stone fails to pass.
Clinical Course: The pain will peak during meals and subside between them. The stone may pop out spontaneously into the mouth, which will immediately resolve the swelling.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your tongue gets pushed up, your throat feels tight, or you develop a high fever, the blocked gland has become severely infected, and you must call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A calcium stone has formed and blocked the tube that carries saliva from your jaw gland to your mouth. When you eat, the gland makes saliva, but it has nowhere to go, causing severe pressure and swelling.
Actionable Steps: Use sour candies to force the gland to make saliva, which helps flush the stone forward. Massage the swelling gently forward toward your chin. Drink plenty of water.

SMS INFORMATION

